{"id":871,"date":"2026-03-07T12:37:55","date_gmt":"2026-03-07T12:37:55","guid":{"rendered":"http:\/\/127.0.0.1:10005\/?p=871"},"modified":"2026-03-08T07:23:37","modified_gmt":"2026-03-08T07:23:37","slug":"%e5%a4%9a%e5%b0%8f%e5%8c%ba%e7%b3%bb%e7%bb%9f%e4%b8%ad%e5%8f%af%e9%87%8d%e6%9e%84%e6%99%ba%e8%83%bd%e8%a1%a8%e9%9d%a2%e8%be%85%e5%8a%a9%e7%9a%84%e7%89%a9%e7%90%86%e5%b1%82%e5%af%86%e9%92%a5%e7%94%9f","status":"publish","type":"post","link":"http:\/\/localhost:10011\/en\/%e5%a4%9a%e5%b0%8f%e5%8c%ba%e7%b3%bb%e7%bb%9f%e4%b8%ad%e5%8f%af%e9%87%8d%e6%9e%84%e6%99%ba%e8%83%bd%e8%a1%a8%e9%9d%a2%e8%be%85%e5%8a%a9%e7%9a%84%e7%89%a9%e7%90%86%e5%b1%82%e5%af%86%e9%92%a5%e7%94%9f\/","title":{"rendered":"Reconfigurable Intelligent Surface-Assisted Physical Layer Secret Key Generation in Multi-Cell Systems"},"content":{"rendered":"<div class=\"wp-block-group has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-3749991f wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\" style=\"min-height:1000px\">\n<div class=\"wp-block-group is-vertical is-content-justification-center is-layout-flex wp-container-core-group-is-layout-458a08b5 wp-block-group-is-layout-flex\"><h2 class=\"lab-paper-title wp-block-post-title\">Reconfigurable Intelligent Surface-Assisted Physical Layer Secret Key Generation in Multi-Cell Systems<\/h2>\n\n\n<p class=\"lab-paper-meta has-small-font-size wp-block-paragraph\">L. Hu, C. Sun, G. Li, A. Hu, and D. W. K. Ng<\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"lab-paper-meta has-small-font-size wp-block-paragraph\"><em>IEEE Transactions on Communications<\/em><\/p>\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group is-nowrap is-layout-flex wp-container-core-group-is-layout-3a88641f wp-block-group-is-layout-flex\">\n<div class=\"wp-block-buttons lab-btn-group is-layout-flex wp-block-buttons-is-layout-flex\">\n<div class=\"wp-block-button\"><a class=\"wp-block-button__link wp-element-button\" href=\"http:\/\/localhost:10011\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/03\/Reconfigurable_Intelligent_Surface-Aided_Secret_Key_Generation_in_Multi-Cell_Systems.pdf\">\ud83d\udcc4 PDF<\/a><\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-button is-style-fill\"><a class=\"wp-block-button__link wp-element-button\" href=\"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1109\/TCOMM.2023.3301048\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1109\/LWC.2023.3330809 noreferrer noopener\">\ud83d\udd17 DOI Link<\/a><\/div>\n<\/div>\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<hr class=\"wp-block-separator has-text-color has-contrast-3-color has-alpha-channel-opacity has-contrast-3-background-color has-background is-style-wide\" style=\"margin-top:var(--wp--preset--spacing--20);margin-bottom:var(--wp--preset--spacing--20)\"\/>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group is-layout-flow wp-block-group-is-layout-flow\">\n<div class=\"wp-block-uagb-advanced-heading uagb-block-46005010 lab-subheading\"><h3 class=\"uagb-heading-text\">\ud83d\udccc Abstract<\/h3><\/div>\n\n\n\n<figure class=\"wp-block-pullquote has-text-align-left lab-abstract-quote has-small-font-size\" style=\"margin-right:var(--wp--preset--spacing--10);margin-left:var(--wp--preset--spacing--10);padding-top:0;padding-right:0;padding-bottom:0;padding-left:0\"><blockquote><p>Physical Layer Secret Key Generation (PKG) exploits the reciprocity and randomness of wireless channels to generate symmetric keys between two legitimate communicating parties. However, in multi-cell systems, PKG suffers from severe pilot contamination due to the reuse of identical pilots across different cells. To address this issue, this paper introduces multiple Reconfigurable Intelligent Surfaces to adaptively shape the propagation environment and enhance PKG performance. Specifically, an optimization problem is formulated to maximize the weighted sum secret key rate by jointly optimizing the precoding matrices at the base stations and the phase shifts at the RISs. To tackle the non-convexity of this problem, an alternating optimization-based algorithm is adopted, decomposing the joint optimization problem into two subproblems: for the precoding matrix subproblem, a Lagrangian dual method based on Karush-Kuhn-Tucker conditions is employed; for the phase shift subproblem, a projected gradient ascent algorithm is utilized. Simulation results validate the effectiveness of the proposed scheme, demonstrating significant improvements in WSKR. Furthermore, compared with the single-RIS scheme, deploying multiple RISs provides spatial diversity, thereby further improving multi-cell PKG performance.<\/p><\/blockquote><\/figure>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-uagb-advanced-heading uagb-block-6adec98e lab-subheading\"><h3 class=\"uagb-heading-text\">\ud83d\udca1 Core Contributions<\/h3><\/div>\n\n\n\n<ul start=\"1\" style=\"border-style:none;border-width:0px\" class=\"wp-block-list lab-feature-list\">\n<li class=\"lab-feature-list\"><strong>Proposed a novel RIS-assisted multi-cell PKG framework<\/strong>: Based on the precoding matrices at the BSs and the phase shifts at the RISs, a complete multi-cell physical layer secret key generation framework is constructed, closed-form expressions for the secret key rate are derived, and the problem is formulated as a network-wide WSKR maximization optimization problem. Additionally, it is theoretically proven that in the absence of RIS deployment, inter-cell interference leads to extremely low KGR, approaching zero.<\/li>\n\n\n\n<li class=\"lab-feature-list\"><strong>Designed an efficient AO algorithm for solving the two subproblems<\/strong>: For the original non-convex optimization problem, the joint optimization is decomposed into two alternately solved subproblems. For the precoding matrix subproblem, the optimal structure is derived based on KKT conditions, and the Lagrangian multipliers are solved via bisection search; for the phase shift vector subproblem, a closed-form gradient expression of the objective function with respect to the phase shifts is derived, and the PGA algorithm is employed for iterative updates with step sizes determined by backtracking line search, ensuring monotonic non-decreasing convergence of the objective value.<\/li>\n\n\n\n<li class=\"lab-feature-list\"><strong>Revealed the spatial diversity gain of distributed multiple RISs<\/strong>: Through systematic simulations in a seven-cell scenario, the impact of RIS deployment locations and numbers on PKG performance is investigated. Simulation results show that deploying RISs at cell boundaries most effectively suppresses inter-cell pilot contamination; compared with the single-RIS scheme, distributed deployment of multiple RISs at cell boundaries provides richer spatial diversity, improving the network-wide WSKR while enhancing the KGR fairness among cells.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-uagb-advanced-heading uagb-block-2f9d57f4 lab-subheading\"><h3 class=\"uagb-heading-text\">\ud83d\udd27 Method Overview<\/h3><\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group lab-paper-results is-nowrap is-layout-flex wp-container-core-group-is-layout-3a88641f wp-block-group-is-layout-flex\">\n<div class=\"wp-block-uagb-image uagb-block-b46d2053 wp-block-uagb-image--layout-default wp-block-uagb-image--effect-static wp-block-uagb-image--align-none wp-container-content-9cfa9a5a\"><figure class=\"wp-block-uagb-image__figure\"><img decoding=\"async\" srcset=\"http:\/\/localhost:10011\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/03\/PixPin_2026-03-08_15-17-22.png ,http:\/\/localhost:10011\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/03\/PixPin_2026-03-08_15-17-22.png 780w, http:\/\/localhost:10011\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/03\/PixPin_2026-03-08_15-17-22.png 360w\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 480px) 150px\" src=\"http:\/\/localhost:10011\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/03\/PixPin_2026-03-08_15-17-22.png\" alt=\"\" class=\"uag-image-985\" width=\"802\" height=\"656\" title=\"PixPin_2026-03-08_15-17-22\" loading=\"lazy\" role=\"img\"\/><\/figure><\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group is-vertical is-layout-flex wp-container-core-group-is-layout-831b2db5 wp-block-group-is-layout-flex\">\n<p class=\"lab-experiment-caption has-medium-font-size wp-block-paragraph\">The proposed framework consists of three sequential stages.<strong>Parameter design stage<\/strong>, where each BS jointly designs the precoding matrix <math xmlns=\"http:\/\/www.w3.org\/1998\/Math\/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">P<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application\/x-tex\">\\mathbf{P}_k<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math> and the RIS phase shift vector <math xmlns=\"http:\/\/www.w3.org\/1998\/Math\/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">v<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application\/x-tex\">\\mathbf{v}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>based on statistical channel state information (statistical CSI), and the phase shift configurations are delivered to each RIS via the smart controller.<strong>Channel probing stage<\/strong>, where the BS and UT alternately transmit pilots under the TDD protocol, and the RISs regulate reflected signals through optimized phase shifts, causing inter-cell pilot interference from other cells to undergo destructive superposition at the target communication nodes to suppress interference power, thereby extracting reciprocal channel features in uplink and downlink channel estimation.<strong>Secret key generation stage<\/strong>, where the BS and UT quantize the obtained channel estimates to generate raw key bit sequences, followed by information reconciliation and privacy amplification, ultimately producing the symmetric key <math xmlns=\"http:\/\/www.w3.org\/1998\/Math\/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>\u03ba<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application\/x-tex\">\\kappa_k<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<\/div>\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-uagb-advanced-heading uagb-block-72007c97 lab-subheading\"><h3 class=\"uagb-heading-text\">\ud83d\udcca Experimental Results<\/h3><\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group lab-paper-results is-nowrap is-layout-flex wp-container-core-group-is-layout-3a88641f wp-block-group-is-layout-flex\">\n<div class=\"wp-block-uagb-image uagb-block-01e98235 wp-block-uagb-image--layout-default wp-block-uagb-image--effect-static wp-block-uagb-image--align-none wp-container-content-9cfa9a5a\"><figure class=\"wp-block-uagb-image__figure\"><img decoding=\"async\" srcset=\"http:\/\/localhost:10011\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/03\/22222.png ,http:\/\/localhost:10011\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/03\/22222.png 780w, http:\/\/localhost:10011\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/03\/22222.png 360w\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 480px) 150px\" src=\"http:\/\/localhost:10011\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/03\/22222.png\" alt=\"\" class=\"uag-image-986\" width=\"802\" height=\"656\" title=\"22222\" loading=\"lazy\" role=\"img\"\/><\/figure><\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group is-vertical is-layout-flex wp-container-core-group-is-layout-831b2db5 wp-block-group-is-layout-flex\">\n<p class=\"lab-experiment-caption has-medium-font-size wp-block-paragraph\"><strong>WSKR is severely limited without RIS, making RIS deployment indispensable<\/strong>: In the seven-cell simulation scenario, the WSKR of the no-RIS scheme saturates at only <strong>5 bits\/channel use<\/strong>in the high transmit power regime, primarily because non-reciprocal inter-cell interference dominates uplink and downlink channel estimation, severely degrading key consistency (BDR saturates at approximately 0.34). The proposed joint optimization algorithm consistently improves WSKR, significantly outperforming all baseline schemes.<\/p>\n<\/div>\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group lab-paper-results is-nowrap is-layout-flex wp-container-core-group-is-layout-3a88641f wp-block-group-is-layout-flex\">\n<div class=\"wp-block-uagb-image uagb-block-01e98235 wp-block-uagb-image--layout-default wp-block-uagb-image--effect-static wp-block-uagb-image--align-none wp-container-content-9cfa9a5a\"><figure class=\"wp-block-uagb-image__figure\"><img decoding=\"async\" srcset=\"http:\/\/localhost:10011\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/03\/PixPin_2026-03-08_15-20-10.png ,http:\/\/localhost:10011\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/03\/PixPin_2026-03-08_15-20-10.png 780w, http:\/\/localhost:10011\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/03\/PixPin_2026-03-08_15-20-10.png 360w\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 480px) 150px\" src=\"http:\/\/localhost:10011\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/03\/PixPin_2026-03-08_15-20-10.png\" alt=\"\" class=\"uag-image-987\" width=\"802\" height=\"656\" title=\"PixPin_2026-03-08_15-20-10\" loading=\"lazy\" role=\"img\"\/><\/figure><\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group is-vertical is-layout-flex wp-container-core-group-is-layout-831b2db5 wp-block-group-is-layout-flex\">\n<p class=\"lab-experiment-caption has-medium-font-size wp-block-paragraph\"><strong>Distributed multiple RISs outperform a single RIS<\/strong>: Under the same total number of RIS elements (both 180), deploying six RISs (30 elements each) distributed at the boundaries of six cells, compared with centralized deployment of a single RIS (180 elements), improves the network-wide WSKR from <strong>19 bits\/channel use<\/strong> to <strong>39 bits\/channel use<\/strong>, and the minimum KGR among cells also increases from 0.25 to 0.35 bits\/channel use, with significantly improved fairness.<\/p>\n<\/div>\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-uagb-advanced-heading uagb-block-e3c2d50d lab-subheading\"><h3 class=\"uagb-heading-text\">\ud83d\udcdd Citation<\/h3><\/div>\n\n\n\n<pre class=\"wp-block-code lab-bibtex-code has-small-font-size\"><code>@article{hu2023reconfigurable,\n  author  = {Lei Hu and Chen Sun and Guyue Li and Aiqun Hu and Derrick Wing Kwan Ng},\n  title   = {Reconfigurable Intelligent Surface-Aided Secret Key Generation in Multi-Cell Systems},\n  journal = {IEEE Transactions on Communications},\n  volume  = {71},\n  number  = {11},\n  pages   = {6499--6513},\n  month   = nov,\n  year    = {2023},\n  doi     = {10.1109\/TCOMM.2023.3301048}\n}\n<\/code><\/pre>\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<hr class=\"wp-block-separator has-alpha-channel-opacity is-style-wide\" style=\"margin-top:var(--wp--preset--spacing--20);margin-bottom:var(--wp--preset--spacing--20)\"\/>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group is-layout-flow wp-block-group-is-layout-flow\">\n<div style=\"height:100px\" aria-hidden=\"true\" class=\"wp-block-spacer\"><\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group is-content-justification-space-between is-nowrap is-layout-flex wp-container-core-group-is-layout-788841da wp-block-group-is-layout-flex\">\n\n<\/div>\n<\/div>\n<\/div>","protected":false},"excerpt":{"rendered":"<p>In multi-cell systems, PKG suffers from severe pilot contamination due to the reuse of identical pilots across different cells. To this end, this paper introduces multiple reconfigurable intelligent surfaces (RIS) to adaptively shape the propagation environment and enhance PKG performance.<\/p>","protected":false},"author":1,"featured_media":903,"comment_status":"open","ping_status":"open","sticky":false,"template":"mypost","format":"standard","meta":{"_uag_custom_page_level_css":"","footnotes":""},"categories":[5],"tags":[],"class_list":["post-871","post","type-post","status-publish","format-standard","has-post-thumbnail","hentry","category-research"],"uagb_featured_image_src":{"full":["http:\/\/localhost:10011\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/03\/PixPin_2026-03-07_21-35-18.png",934,508,false],"thumbnail":["http:\/\/localhost:10011\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/03\/PixPin_2026-03-07_21-35-18-150x150.png",150,150,true],"medium":["http:\/\/localhost:10011\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/03\/PixPin_2026-03-07_21-35-18-300x163.png",300,163,true],"medium_large":["http:\/\/localhost:10011\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/03\/PixPin_2026-03-07_21-35-18-768x418.png",768,418,true],"large":["http:\/\/localhost:10011\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/03\/PixPin_2026-03-07_21-35-18.png",934,508,false],"1536x1536":["http:\/\/localhost:10011\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/03\/PixPin_2026-03-07_21-35-18.pn